 /wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Hernan-Diaz-c-Pascal-Perich_for-website.jpg
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://inprinthouston.org/wp-content/uploads/202
 6/07/Hernan-Diaz-c-Pascal-Perich_for-website.jpg
UID:nP1AYid-3dUUAneFuMntZ
SUMMARY:Inprint Hernan Diaz Reading
DTSTAMP:20260818T032835Z
DTSTART:20261012T193000Z
DESCRIPTION:General admission tickets will go on sale on Monday\, September
	 21\, 2026 at 12 pm. Season tickets are on sale here. \nHernan Diaz will r
	ead from his new novel Ply\, followed by an on-stage conversation led by a
	 local author/journalist. The evening will conclude with a book sale and s
	igning. The event is presented as part of the 2026/2027 Inprint Margarett 
	Root Brown Reading Series.  \nDescribed by Lauren Groff as a “narrative ge
	nius\,” Hernan Diaz is the Pulitzer Prize-winning and bestselling author o
	f the novel Trust\, named one of the New York Times’s Best 100 Books of th
	e 21st Century and currently being developed as a limited series for HBO. 
	His literary work is celebrated for its genre-bending reinvention of Ameri
	can mythology and its complex exploration of wealth\, capitalism\, and iso
	lation. His first novel\, In the Distance\, was a finalist for the Pulitze
	r Prize and the PEN/Faulkner Award. His work has been translated into 37 l
	anguages. \nAfter rewriting America’s past in his two previous novels\, fr
	om 1920s Wall Street to the 19th century western frontier\, Diaz turns to 
	the future with his new novel Ply. A combination of Dickensian odyssey\, f
	amily drama\, and scientific thriller\, Ply examines the place of technolo
	gy in the American imagination. Centuries from now\, the state has been di
	smantled\, and humans are reinventing social bonds and learning new ways t
	o coexist with nature. Following a childhood defined by loss\, survival\, 
	and found family\, an orphan grows up to become a “pincher\,” someone who 
	steals electricity from the grid to sell it on the black market. “Diaz bri
	lliantly envisions how love\, trust\, and loyalty can remain when freedom 
	and justice have collapsed and makes every page exhilarating and meaningfu
	l” (Library Journal\, starred review). \n\n\nhttps://inprinthouston.org/ev
	ent/inprint-hernan-diaz-reading/\n\nFrom Inprint Houston
URL:https://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-hernan-diaz-reading/
LOCATION:Stude Concert Hall\, Rice University\, Entrance #18 & #20\, 6100 M
	ain St.\, Houston\, TX\, 77005\, United States
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:inprint-houston
CATEGORIES:Inprint Houston
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT2H
END:VEVENT
